### Endogenic Cycle
:::

:::.d-none d-lg-inline-block .col-lg-2
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Soil%20and%20Enviromental%20Chemistry/02.Matter%20and%20Cycles%20of%20Matter/assets/endogenic_cycle_cover_img.png)
:::
:::



:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
**Endogenic cycle** refers to the `cycling and redistribution` of matter within the Earth that is driven mainly by `internal heat and geological processes`. 

It operates primarily within the **geosphere** and connects the Earth's crust, mantle, and surface through processes such as **plate tectonics, volcanism, uplift, metamorphism, melting, and rock formation**.

Unlike biogeochemical cycles, which are strongly driven by `biological and atmospheric processes`, the endogenic cycle is powered largely by `Earth's internal energy`.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Soil%20and%20Enviromental%20Chemistry/02.Matter%20and%20Cycles%20of%20Matter/assets/endogenic_cycle_sequence_diagram.png)
:::
:::

