### Magmatism

Heat inside the Earth causes rocks to melt and form **magma**.

```text
Heat + pressure
      ↓
Partial melting of rocks
      ↓
     Magma
```

Magma may remain underground or rise toward the Earth's surface.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-4
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Soil%20and%20Enviromental%20Chemistry/02.Matter%20and%20Cycles%20of%20Matter/assets/megmatism_diagram.png)
:::
:::


### Volcanism

When magma reaches the Earth's surface, it is released as **lava, volcanic gases, and ash**.

```text
Magma
  ↓
Volcanic eruption
  ↓
Lava + gases + ash
  ↓
New igneous rocks
```

Volcanism transfers materials from the **interior of Earth to the surface**.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Plate Tectonics

The movement of tectonic plates redistributes rocks and minerals across the Earth's crust.

![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Soil%20and%20Enviromental%20Chemistry/02.Matter%20and%20Cycles%20of%20Matter/assets/plate_tectonics_diagram.png)

Major processes include:

* **Convergence** — plates move toward each other.
* **Divergence** — plates move apart.
* **Subduction** — one plate moves beneath another.
* **Uplift** — rocks are brought toward the surface.

### Subduction

At convergent plate boundaries, oceanic crust can sink into the mantle.

```text
Oceanic crust
     ↓
Subduction
     ↓
     Mantle
     ↓
Heating + melting
     ↓
     Magma
```

This process returns surface materials to the **Earth's interior**.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Metamorphism

Existing rocks undergo changes because of `high temperature, pressure`, and chemically `active fluids` without completely `!melting`.

```text
Existing rock
     ↓
Heat + pressure
     ↓
Metamorphic rock
```
</md></sn><sn><md>:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
### Uplift

Geological forces can **move rocks** from `deeper parts of the Earth toward the surface`.

```text
Deep rocks
    ↓
Tectonic uplift
    ↓
Surface rocks
```

Once exposed, these rocks become available for **weathering, erosion, and sediment formation**, linking the endogenic cycle with surface processes.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Soil%20and%20Enviromental%20Chemistry/02.Matter%20and%20Cycles%20of%20Matter/assets/uplift.png)
:::
